A **GraphQL schema** defines the structure of your API - what data can be queried and how it's organized.

**Core Schema Types:**
- **Query** - Read operations (required)
- **Mutation** - Write operations (optional)
- **Subscription** - Real-time updates (optional)

```graphql
# Schema Definition
type Query {
  user(id: ID!): User
  posts: [Post!]!
}

type Mutation {
  createUser(input: CreateUserInput!): User!
  updateUser(id: ID!, input: UpdateUserInput!): User!
}

type Subscription {
  postAdded: Post!
  userUpdated(id: ID!): User!
}

# Custom Types
type User {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  email: String!
  posts: [Post!]!
}

type Post {
  id: ID!
  title: String!
  content: String!
  author: User!
}
```

**Queries** are read operations to fetch data from the server. They specify exactly what fields you want to retrieve.

```graphql
# Basic query
query {
  users {
    id
    name
    email
  }
}

# Query with parameters
query GetUser($userId: ID!) {
  user(id: $userId) {
    id
    name
    email
    posts {
      title
      createdAt
    }
  }
}

# Nested query with aliases
query {
  currentUser: user(id: "123") {
    name
    email
  }
  allPosts: posts {
    id
    title
    author {
      name
    }
  }
}
```

**Mutations** are write operations that modify data on the server (create, update, delete).

```graphql
# Define mutation in schema
type Mutation {
  createUser(input: CreateUserInput!): User!
  updateUser(id: ID!, input: UpdateUserInput!): User!
  deleteUser(id: ID!): Boolean!
}

input CreateUserInput {
  name: String!
  email: String!
  password: String!
}

# Execute mutation
mutation CreateNewUser($input: CreateUserInput!) {
  createUser(input: $input) {
    id
    name
    email
    createdAt
  }
}

# Variables
{
  "input": {
    "name": "John Doe",
    "email": "john@example.com",
    "password": "securePassword123"
  }
}

# Multiple mutations in one request
mutation {
  createUser(input: { name: "Alice", email: "alice@example.com" }) {
    id
    name
  }
  createPost(input: { title: "Hello World", authorId: "123" }) {
    id
    title
  }
}
```

**Resolvers** are functions that fetch/compute data for each field in your schema. They connect your schema to your data sources.

```javascript
// Resolver structure: (parent, args, context, info) => data

const resolvers = {
  // Query resolvers
  Query: {
    user: (parent, { id }, context) => {
      return context.db.users.findById(id);
    },
    posts: (parent, args, context) => {
      return context.db.posts.findAll();
    }
  },

  // Mutation resolvers
  Mutation: {
    createUser: async (parent, { input }, context) => {
      const user = await context.db.users.create(input);
      return user;
    }
  },

  // Type resolvers (for nested fields)
  User: {
    posts: (parent, args, context) => {
      // parent = the user object
      return context.db.posts.findByAuthorId(parent.id);
    },
    fullName: (parent) => {
      return `${parent.firstName} ${parent.lastName}`;
    }
  },

  Post: {
    author: (parent, args, context) => {
      return context.db.users.findById(parent.authorId);
    }
  }
};

// Resolver execution flow:
// 1. Query: user(id: "123") → calls Query.user resolver
// 2. User.posts → calls User.posts resolver with user as parent
// 3. Post.author → calls Post.author resolver with post as parent
```

GraphQL has built-in error handling that returns errors alongside data in the response.

```javascript
// Throwing errors in resolvers
const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    user: async (parent, { id }, context) => {
      try {
        const user = await context.db.users.findById(id);
        if (!user) {
          throw new Error('User not found');
        }
        return user;
      } catch (error) {
        throw new Error(`Failed to fetch user: ${error.message}`);
      }
    }
  }
};

// Custom error types
class NotFoundError extends Error {
  constructor(message) {
    super(message);
    this.code = 'NOT_FOUND';
  }
}

class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message, field) {
    super(message);
    this.code = 'VALIDATION_ERROR';
    this.field = field;
  }
}

// Error response format
{
  "data": {
    "user": null
  },
  "errors": [
    {
      "message": "User not found",
      "locations": [{ "line": 2, "column": 3 }],
      "path": ["user"]
    }
  ]
}

// Partial success with errors
{
  "data": {
    "users": [
      { "id": "1", "name": "John" },
      null  // This user failed to load
    ]
  },
  "errors": [
    {
      "message": "Access denied for user 2",
      "path": ["users", 1]
    }
  ]
}
```

A **GraphQL server** processes GraphQL queries and returns data. It acts as a layer between clients and data sources.

**Key Responsibilities:**
- **Parse & validate** queries against schema
- **Execute resolvers** to fetch data
- **Format responses** with data and errors
- **Handle subscriptions** for real-time updates

```javascript
// Basic GraphQL server setup (Node.js + Apollo Server)
const { ApolloServer } = require('apollo-server');
const { typeDefs, resolvers } = require('./schema');

const server = new ApolloServer({
  typeDefs,
  resolvers,
  context: ({ req }) => ({
    user: getUser(req.headers.authorization),
    db: database,
    dataSources: {
      userAPI: new UserAPI(),
      postAPI: new PostAPI()
    }
  })
});

server.listen(4000).then(({ url }) => {
  console.log(`Server ready at ${url}`);
});

// Server processing flow:
// 1. Receive query from client
// 2. Parse query syntax
// 3. Validate against schema
// 4. Execute resolvers
// 5. Format and return response
```

**Queries** are one-time requests for data, while **Subscriptions** maintain persistent connections for real-time updates.

```graphql
# QUERY - One-time data fetch
query GetMessages {
  messages {
    id
    text
    user {
      name
    }
    createdAt
  }
}

# SUBSCRIPTION - Real-time updates
subscription MessageAdded {
  messageAdded {
    id
    text
    user {
      name
    }
    createdAt
  }
}

# Subscription with parameters
subscription MessagesByRoom($roomId: ID!) {
  messageAdded(roomId: $roomId) {
    id
    text
    user {
      name
    }
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Server-side subscription resolver
const { PubSub } = require('apollo-server');
const pubsub = new PubSub();

const resolvers = {
  Subscription: {
    messageAdded: {
      subscribe: () => pubsub.asyncIterator(['MESSAGE_ADDED'])
    },
    messagesByRoom: {
      subscribe: (parent, { roomId }) => 
        pubsub.asyncIterator([`MESSAGE_ADDED_${roomId}`])
    }
  },

  Mutation: {
    sendMessage: async (parent, { input }) => {
      const message = await createMessage(input);

      // Trigger subscription
      pubsub.publish('MESSAGE_ADDED', { 
        messageAdded: message 
      });

      return message;
    }
  }
};

// Client-side subscription (JavaScript)
const subscription = client.subscribe({
  query: gql`
    subscription {
      messageAdded {
        id
        text
        user { name }
      }
    }
  `
}).subscribe({
  next: (data) => {
    console.log('New message:', data.messageAdded);
  }
});
```

GraphQL supports multiple pagination patterns: **limit/offset**, **cursor-based**, and **Relay-style connections**.

```graphql
# 1. LIMIT/OFFSET Pagination (simple but has issues)
type Query {
  posts(limit: Int, offset: Int): [Post!]!
  postsCount: Int!
}

query GetPosts($limit: Int!, $offset: Int!) {
  posts(limit: $limit, offset: $offset) {
    id
    title
    content
  }
  postsCount
}

# 2. CURSOR-BASED Pagination (recommended)
type Query {
  posts(first: Int, after: String, last: Int, before: String): PostConnection!
}

type PostConnection {
  edges: [PostEdge!]!
  pageInfo: PageInfo!
  totalCount: Int!
}

type PostEdge {
  node: Post!
  cursor: String!
}

type PageInfo {
  hasNextPage: Boolean!
  hasPreviousPage: Boolean!
  startCursor: String
  endCursor: String
}

# Query with cursor pagination
query GetPosts($first: Int!, $after: String) {
  posts(first: $first, after: $after) {
    edges {
      node {
        id
        title
        content
      }
      cursor
    }
    pageInfo {
      hasNextPage
      endCursor
    }
    totalCount
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Resolver implementation for cursor pagination
const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    posts: async (parent, { first, after, last, before }, context) => {
      const limit = first || last || 10;
      const offset = after ? decodeCursor(after) : 0;

      const posts = await context.db.posts.findMany({
        limit: limit + 1, // Get one extra to check hasNextPage
        offset
      });

      const hasNextPage = posts.length > limit;
      if (hasNextPage) posts.pop(); // Remove extra post

      const edges = posts.map((post, index) => ({
        node: post,
        cursor: encodeCursor(offset + index)
      }));

      return {
        edges,
        pageInfo: {
          hasNextPage,
          hasPreviousPage: offset > 0,
          startCursor: edges[0]?.cursor,
          endCursor: edges[edges.length - 1]?.cursor
        },
        totalCount: await context.db.posts.count()
      };
    }
  }
};

// Cursor encoding/decoding
function encodeCursor(index) {
  return Buffer.from(index.toString()).toString('base64');
}

function decodeCursor(cursor) {
  return parseInt(Buffer.from(cursor, 'base64').toString());
}
```

**Fragments** are reusable pieces of query logic that help avoid duplication and make queries more maintainable.

```graphql
# Define fragments
fragment UserBasicInfo on User {
  id
  name
  email
  avatar
}

fragment PostSummary on Post {
  id
  title
  excerpt
  createdAt
  author {
    ...UserBasicInfo
  }
}

# Use fragments in queries
query GetUserProfile($userId: ID!) {
  user(id: $userId) {
    ...UserBasicInfo
    bio
    joinedAt
    posts {
      ...PostSummary
      commentsCount
    }
  }
}

query GetPosts {
  posts {
    ...PostSummary
    tags {
      name
    }
  }
}

# Inline fragments for union types
query GetFeedItems {
  feedItems {
    ... on Post {
      title
      content
      author { name }
    }
    ... on Video {
      title
      duration
      thumbnail
    }
    ... on Photo {
      caption
      imageUrl
      photographer { name }
    }
  }
}

# Fragment with variables
fragment UserPosts on User {
  posts(first: $postLimit) {
    edges {
      node {
        id
        title
      }
    }
  }
}

query GetUserWithPosts($userId: ID!, $postLimit: Int!) {
  user(id: $userId) {
    name
    ...UserPosts
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Using fragments in JavaScript/TypeScript
import { gql } from '@apollo/client';

// Define fragment
const USER_BASIC_INFO = gql`
  fragment UserBasicInfo on User {
    id
    name
    email
    avatar
  }
`;

// Use in queries
const GET_USER_PROFILE = gql`
  ${USER_BASIC_INFO}

  query GetUserProfile($userId: ID!) {
    user(id: $userId) {
      ...UserBasicInfo
      bio
      joinedAt
    }
  }
`;

const GET_USERS = gql`
  ${USER_BASIC_INFO}

  query GetUsers {
    users {
      ...UserBasicInfo
      postsCount
    }
  }
`;
```

**Benefits of Fragments:**
- ✅ **Reusability** - Share common field sets
- ✅ **Maintainability** - Update in one place
- ✅ **Type Safety** - Fragments are type-specific
- ✅ **Performance** - Avoid duplicate fields
- ✅ **Organization** - Keep queries clean and focused

**Subscriptions** enable real-time communication by maintaining a persistent connection between client and server for live data updates.

```graphql
# Schema definition
type Subscription {
  messageAdded(roomId: ID!): Message!
  userOnline: User!
  postUpdated(postId: ID!): Post!
}

type Message {
  id: ID!
  content: String!
  user: User!
  room: Room!
  createdAt: String!
}

# Client subscription
subscription MessageAdded($roomId: ID!) {
  messageAdded(roomId: $roomId) {
    id
    content
    user {
      name
      avatar
    }
    createdAt
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Server-side resolver
const { PubSub } = require('apollo-server');
const pubsub = new PubSub();

const resolvers = {
  Subscription: {
    messageAdded: {
      subscribe: (parent, { roomId }) => 
        pubsub.asyncIterator([`MESSAGE_ADDED_${roomId}`])
    }
  },

  Mutation: {
    sendMessage: async (parent, { input }) => {
      const message = await createMessage(input);
      pubsub.publish(`MESSAGE_ADDED_${input.roomId}`, {
        messageAdded: message
      });
      return message;
    }
  }
};
```

